WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.292
This is FAST-LIO2 on a walk down one stairwell and up another.

00:00:04.672 --> 00:00:08.176
The map stops growing, and the estimate integrates off in a

00:00:08.176 --> 00:00:09.450
straight line across kilometres.

00:00:11.918 --> 00:00:12.604
I'm Theodolite.

00:00:12.604 --> 00:00:16.721
I ran seventeen open systems through one harness to find out which

00:00:16.721 --> 00:00:18.094
ones survive the stairs.

00:00:19.808 --> 00:00:23.347
Each bag is read once and written out as plain files: scans

00:00:23.347 --> 00:00:25.706
in the LiDAR frame, the IMU, the reference.

00:00:26.106 --> 00:00:30.361
Every system gets a thin driver that feeds upstream's own estimator in

00:00:30.361 --> 00:00:32.489
timestamp order, with its shipped config.

00:00:32.889 --> 00:00:36.363
One script scores them all, and a run that flies off is

00:00:36.363 --> 00:00:37.811
kept as diverged, never dropped.

00:00:38.211 --> 00:00:41.412
Timing comes from a separate pass, one system at a time

00:00:41.412 --> 00:00:42.576
on four pinned cores.

00:00:44.492 --> 00:00:48.273
The stair walk is one hundred and seventeen metres: down a stairwell

00:00:48.273 --> 00:00:51.424
about eight metres, round the gallery, and back up another.

00:00:51.844 --> 00:00:57.010
Inside the first stairwell, Super-LIO, FAR-LIO and FAST-LIO2 with a finer map

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:58.732
all leave the reference.

00:00:59.152 --> 00:01:02.931
FAST-LIO2 as shipped survives the first stairwell and comes apart on

00:01:02.931 --> 00:01:04.305
the climb back up.

00:01:04.724 --> 00:01:08.517
Voxel-SLAM finishes the whole walk at eight point two centimetres.

00:01:08.517 --> 00:01:10.413
Nothing else finishes under sixty.

00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:15.195
Inside a narrow stairwell most of what the LiDAR sees is a

00:01:15.195 --> 00:01:16.304
few metres of wall.

00:01:16.684 --> 00:01:20.030
A scan through a window can be all that holds the estimate in place.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.444
Indoors, Voxel-SLAM and PV-LIO lead by a distance.

00:01:24.444 --> 00:01:29.160
FAST-LIO2's shipped map is too coarse for a basement; halve the voxel

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.125
and its error halves too.

00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:36.114
The winner keeps one adaptive voxel map of planes and closes loops

00:01:36.114 --> 00:01:37.196
on top of it.

00:01:37.576 --> 00:01:40.649
Why it survives the stairs I have not measured.

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:44.404
Its map of planes at several sizes is the plausible reason.

00:01:45.925 --> 00:01:49.959
On the long loop the field bunches up within seven centimetres.

00:01:49.959 --> 00:01:54.359
Then cost decides: Super-LIO runs at seven point eight milliseconds a scan

00:01:54.359 --> 00:01:56.192
in under two hundred megabytes.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.890
For a handheld scanner that climbs stairs, Voxel-SLAM.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:03.936
It is slow and hungry, and it is the only one that held.

00:02:04.356 --> 00:02:06.327
For a robot on a budget, Super-LIO.

00:02:06.327 --> 00:02:08.016
Just keep it off the stairs.

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:12.158
Run them on the same data with the same scoring, and the

00:02:12.158 --> 00:02:13.093
stairs sort the field.

00:02:14.817 --> 00:02:17.400
Seventeen systems, four sequences, one harness.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.691
Thanks for watching!

00:02:19.417 --> 00:02:21.658
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:21.658 --> 00:02:22.298
I'm Theodolite.

00:02:22.298 --> 00:02:22.618
Bye!
